{#-
Extractors:
  - response: AsIsExtractor()

Branches (wording from docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-20-heritage-create.md):
  - species;
  - race: `species_name`, `species_numbers`, `example_measure` and
    `inherited` set; overrides as a top-level "no_longer_true";
  - character with a heritage: `heritage_label`, `heritage_numbers` and
    `inherited` set; overrides per fact;
  - anything else (a character with no heritage, a place, lore, an event,
    an item).
`inherited` is `[(label, source_name, category, text)]`.

Every branch asks for the thing's "aka" and the "names" its facts use,
and gets the context blocks (wording from
docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-21-connections.md, "Recommended prompt
text"): `mentions` is `[(lead, text)]` and `about` `[(label, facts)]`, a
stub's "Mentioned elsewhere" and "About those"; `named` is
`[(written, label, facts)]`, "Named in this prompt". A label carries the
entry's everyday name: "Tsardom of Moscavia (place, usually called
Moscavia)".

A character with spheres in context (wording from
docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-22-spheres.md, "(c) Ties and exceptions
at character creation") gets the spheres block, `spheres` as
`[(heading, about, [(label, drawer title, line)])]`, the `"ties"` key in
its example object and the ties rules. A sphere shown there isn't also
"Named in this prompt".

The "names" rule names faiths, orders, councils, cultures and fields as
"sphere" with a "template", in the sphere core's wording (spheres probe,
"(a) The core"): the Task 10 session found the old "a group, order or
faction is lore" made a place's state church a lore stub
(docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-22-spheres-flow.md).

The generic branch with `nearby` (a list of candidate lines, "Name (kind;
usually called X; part of Y, in its Z drawer; drawers: ...; stub): its
summary or first fact") gets the "Entries near this one" block, the
"elsewhere" list in its example object and the elsewhere rules, verbatim
from docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-22-curation.md, "Wording, if the
owner goes ahead" (create-thing, v3: with the relation-claim rule). The
probe's held-by rule isn't here (probe 4 fell back to tagging by hand),
though the example carries "held_by": null as probed.

The generic branch with `broader` (`[(label, text)]`, the broader entry's
facts labelled M1..., a dated one led "(In 994)" or "(Since 994)", and
`broader_name`) gets the move-in block after the nearby one, "move_in" in
its example object and its rule after the elsewhere rules, verbatim from
docs/superpowers/probes/2026-10-22-curation.md, "Probe 2: moving in",
"Wording".
-#}
